<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dunnottar Castle</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dunnottar Castle is a ruined fortress on a rocky headland on the north-east coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, south of Stonehaven in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aberdeenshire">Aberdeenshire</a>. The site is identified with Dún Foither, a Pictish stronghold recorded in the Irish annals as besieged in 681 and 694, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a> attacked it around 900. The surviving buildings date mainly from the fourteenth to seventeenth centuries, when it was the seat of the Earls Marischal.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dunnottar-castle.jpg" alt="Dunnottar Castle" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anne Burgess · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dunnottar_Castle_-_geograph.org.uk_-_8057610.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
